::GEO_U06_L01_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.8
Question:
Which evidence supports classifying a coordinate quadrilateral as a parallelogram?
{
~A. One diagonal is longer than one side#That comparison does not prove opposite sides are parallel.
=B. Both pairs of opposite sides have equal slopes#Equal slopes for both pairs of opposite sides support parallel opposite sides.
~C. The figure looks slanted#Appearance is not coordinate proof.
~D. The perimeter is positive#Every polygon perimeter is positive, so this does not classify the figure.
}
::GEO_U06_L01_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.8
Question:
Which calculation supports congruent sides in a coordinate proof?
{
~A. Slope#Slope supports direction, not side length.
~B. Midpoint#Midpoint supports bisection, not length equality by itself.
~C. Area#Area does not prove individual side congruence.
=D. Distance#Distance calculations compare side lengths.
}
::GEO_U06_L01_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.8
Question:
What does a shared midpoint of diagonals support?
{
=A. The diagonals bisect each other#A common midpoint means each diagonal cuts the other in half.
~B. The sides are perpendicular#Perpendicular sides require slope evidence.
~C. The sides are all congruent#Side congruence requires distance evidence.
~D. The figure is always a square#A square requires more evidence than diagonal bisection.
}
::GEO_U06_L01_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.8
Question:
Adjacent side slopes are 3 and -1/3. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. The sides are parallel#Parallel lines have equal slopes.
~B. The sides have equal length#Slope does not give side length.
=C. The sides are perpendicular#Negative reciprocal slopes support perpendicular lines.
~D. The diagonals bisect each other#This requires midpoint evidence.
}
::GEO_U06_L01_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.8
Question:
Which coordinate proof statement is valid?
{
~A. The shape looks like a rhombus, so it is a rhombus#Appearance is not enough evidence.
=B. The slopes of opposite sides are equal, so the opposite sides are parallel#This connects a calculation to a valid geometric conclusion.
~C. The coordinates are large, so the sides are congruent#Coordinate size does not prove side congruence.
~D. The graph is drawn neatly, so the proof is complete#A proof needs mathematical evidence, not neatness.
}
